<ToolLayout title="ROI Calculator — How to Calculate Return on Investment" description="Calculate ROI percentage, net profit, and annualized return from an investment and return value.">
  <RoiCalculator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>How to Calculate ROI (Formula + Example)</h2>
  <p><strong>ROI = (Net Profit ÷ Cost of Investment) × 100.</strong> Net profit is the return you received minus what you originally invested.</p>
  <p><strong>Example:</strong> You invest $10,000 and it grows to $12,000. Net profit is $2,000. ROI = ($2,000 ÷ $10,000) × 100 = <strong>20%</strong>. If that took 6 months rather than a full year, the annualized ROI is closer to 44%, since the same gain achieved twice as fast compounds to a higher yearly rate.</p>

  <h2>Why annualized ROI matters for comparing investments</h2>
  <p>A 20% return sounds great until you realize one investment achieved it in 3 months and another took 3 years — annualizing ROI puts different time horizons on equal footing, which is essential for genuinely comparing investment options rather than being misled by raw percentages.</p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your initial investment and the return value received.</li>
    <li>Enter the time period over which the return occurred.</li>
    <li>See net profit, ROI percentage, and annualized ROI.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A $100,000 investment returning $120,000 over 6 months is a 20% raw ROI, but annualized (compounding over a full year), that's closer to a 44% annualized rate.</p>

  <h2>Common ROI Calculation Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Comparing raw ROI across different time periods</strong> without annualizing, making a short-term investment look worse than a long-term one with the same raw percentage.</li>
    <li><strong>Ignoring risk when comparing ROI</strong> — a higher ROI often comes with higher risk, which a simple ROI number doesn't capture.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting to include all costs</strong> in the initial investment figure, overstating the actual ROI.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This ROI Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Comparing the performance of different investments or projects with different time horizons</li>
    <li>Reporting return on a completed initiative to stakeholders</li>
    <li>Evaluating whether a past investment decision paid off relative to alternatives</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>ROI Calculator FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What is a good ROI?</strong> It depends heavily on the investment type and risk — stock market investors often benchmark against 7-10% annualized as a long-term average, while business or marketing ROI expectations vary widely by industry.</p>
  <p><strong>How do you calculate ROI percentage?</strong> Divide net profit (return minus original investment) by the cost of investment, then multiply by 100. The calculator above does this instantly from your entered numbers.</p>
  <p><strong>Should I always compare annualized ROI?</strong> It's the fairer comparison when time periods differ significantly — for investments over the same time period, raw ROI is equally valid and simpler.</p>
</ToolLayout>
